prompt.text:
Implement a new, self-contained TypeScript entitlement registry.

Export these declarations exactly:

```typescript
export type EntitlementStatus = "enabled" | "revoked";

export interface EntitlementInput {
  id: string;
  product: string;
  units: number;
  intervalSteps: number;
  intervalCount: number;
  lastChargedStep: number | null;
  status: EntitlementStatus;
}

export interface EntitlementView extends EntitlementInput {}

export class EntitlementRegistry {
  constructor(initial?: readonly EntitlementInput[]);
  add(input: EntitlementInput): void;
  list(status?: EntitlementStatus): EntitlementView[];
  revoke(id: string): boolean;
}
```

An ID and product must contain at least one non-whitespace character, although their original strings must be preserved. IDs must be unique. `units` and `lastChargedStep`, when present, must be non-negative integers. `intervalSteps` and `intervalCount` must be positive integers. Reject malformed entries and duplicate IDs with `Error`.

`list` must optionally filter by status and return entries ordered by ascending ID using ordinary string comparison. `revoke` changes an enabled entry to revoked and returns `true`; it returns `false` for an unknown or already-revoked ID. The constructor, `add`, and `list` must not create mutable aliases between caller-owned objects and registry state. An omitted or empty initial array is valid.

